389e-07,0.0,-1.9073468138230965e-06,-3.933898824470816e-06],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,10061,57960,7141,4779,57960,19127,4779,323,57960,32214,3,387,2326,25941,1741,429,57960,7141,488,1124,19127,488,1124,32214,284,1124,2493,2418,220,1416,582,525,2661,429,57960,52591,1124,7141,1124,52591,1124,19127,284,1124,66,2388,1124,37018,35702,2493,15170,18,2137,3,1221,8253,57960,37018,35702,9407,1124,7141,1124,9407,1124,19127,15170,59,9407,1124,32214,92,2418,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nLet $\\alpha,$ $\\beta,$ and $\\gamma$ be three angles such that $\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma = \\pi.$  If we are given that $\\tan \\alpha \\tan \\beta = \\csc \\frac{\\pi}{3},$ then determine $\\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{\\cos \\gamma}.$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"084e62b600ab4e83258614478fb6a0dd769b3fe96e368eb1af1d447749ed3660"},"seconds":58.328924894332886,"seed":528621146912658553,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem, we will use the given information and trigonometric identities step by step.\n\nFirst, note the given condition:\n\\[\n\\tan \\alpha \\tan \\beta = \\csc \\frac{\\pi}{3}\n\\]\nWe know that \\(\\csc \\frac{\\pi}{3} = \\frac{1}{\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{3}} = \\frac{1}{\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}} = \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\). Thus,\n\\[\n\\tan \\alpha \\tan \\beta = \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n\\]\n\nNext, we use the identity for the tangent of the sum of angles:\n\\[\n\\tan(\\alpha + \\beta) = \\tan(\\pi - \\gamma) = -\\tan \\gamma\n\\]\nUsing the tangent addition formula, we have:\n\\[\n\\tan(\\alpha + \\beta) = \\frac{\\tan \\alpha + \\tan \\beta}{1 - \\tan \\alpha \\tan \\beta}\n\\]\nSince \\(\\alpha + \\beta = \\pi - \\gamma\\), we get:\n\\[\n-\\tan \\gamma = \\frac{\\tan \\alpha + \\tan \\beta}{1 - \\tan \\alpha \\tan \\beta}\n\\]\nSubstituting \\(\\tan \\alpha \\tan \\beta = \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\), we get:\n\\[\n-\\tan \\gamma = \\frac{\\tan \\alpha + \\tan \\beta}{1 - \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}} = \\frac{\\tan \\alpha + \\tan \\beta}{\\frac{3 - 2\\sqrt{3}}{3}} = \\frac{3(\\tan \\alpha + \\tan \\beta)}{3 - 2\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]\nThis implies:\n\\[\n\\tan \\gamma = -\\frac{3(\\tan \\alpha + \\tan \\beta)}{3 - 2\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find \\(\\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{\\cos \\gamma}\\). Using the identity \\(\\cos \\gamma = -\\cos(\\alpha + \\beta)\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\cos \\gamma = -\\cos(\\alpha + \\beta) = -(\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta - \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta)\n\\]\nWe know that:\n\\[\n\\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta = \\tan \\alpha \\tan \\beta \\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta = \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta\n\\]\nThus,\n\\[\n\\cos \\gamma = -\\left( \\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta - \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta \\right) = -\\left( \\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta \\left(1 - \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right) \\right) = -\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta \\left( \\frac{3 - 2\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\right)\n\\]\nTherefore,\n\\[\n\\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{\\cos \\gamma} = \\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{-\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta \\left( \\frac{3 - 2\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\right)} = -\\frac{3}{3 - 2\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]\nRationalizing the denominator, we get:\n\\[\n-\\frac{3}{3 - 2\\sqrt{3}} \\cdot \\frac{3 + 2\\sqrt{3}}{3 + 2\\sqrt{3}} = -\\frac{3(3 + 2\\sqrt{3})}{(3)^2 - (2\\sqrt{3})^2} = -\\frac{3(3 + 2\\sqrt{3})}{9 - 12} = -\\frac{3(3 + 2\\sqrt{3})}{-3} = 3 + 2\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{3 + 2\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{3 + 2\\sqrt{3}}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
